      "reasons": {
        "0": "Loss of deep-tendon reflexes and respiratory depression during magnesium therapy can indicate toxicity. Assess respiration, reflexes, urine output, and the current infusion rather than treating these changes as expected sedation.",
        "1": "Stop the magnesium infusion and obtain urgent help while supporting airway and breathing. Follow the toxicity protocol, including prescribed calcium gluconate when indicated; renal function and magnesium exposure also need review.",
        "2": "New weakness, excessive drowsiness, or breathing difficulty may be early signs of clinically important magnesium effects and must be reported promptly. Continued monitoring includes respiratory status, reflexes, and renal clearance.",
        "3": "Recovery of adequate breathing and reflexes with stable clinical observations supports improvement after treatment. Ongoing monitoring is necessary because accumulated magnesium may persist, particularly with reduced renal clearance.",
        "4": "Additional magnesium can worsen neuromuscular blockade and respiratory depression when toxicity is suspected. The infusion should be stopped and the emergency toxicity pathway followed rather than giving more of the suspected cause."
      }
